## Founding & History
Small Wonder Wines is a relatively new brand operating on a mature property in Tasmania's Tamar Valley. The vineyard was originally established in 1998. The site was formerly known as Goaty Hill. In 2020, the property was sold by its founders to the Overstory group after two decades of ownership. The first wines under the Small Wonder brand were released in 2022. A new, purpose-built winery was completed in January 2024, bringing all winemaking on-site. In December 2024, Overstory, the owner of Small Wonder, acquired a second 24-hectare vineyard in the Coal River Valley, a strategic move to expand its footprint and double production volume in Tasmania over the next three years.

## People
Small Wonder is a family-owned Tasmanian wine estate. The winery is owned by the Overstory group. Wayne Nunn is the vineyard manager, and Andrew Trio is the winemaker. Dylan Grigg also serves as a consultant. Robert Stewart is the cellar door manager. Matt Connaugton is one of the vineyard managers for their Coal River Valley site.

## Vineyard & Site
The original Tamar Valley vineyard spans 20 hectares. It is nestled within native bushland on the Western Ridge of the Tamar Valley, at 41 degrees South latitude and 50 meters above sea level. The vineyard was first planted in 1998, with subsequent plantings in 2000, 2001, 2006, 2016, and 2023. The oldest vines are now 27 years old, with an average vine age of around 20 years.

The Tamar Valley site features diverse soil types. Grey sand at the top of the vineyard is suitable for aromatic whites, while grey silt loam transitioning to red silt clay loam and Ferris mudstone on the slopes is ideal for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Riesling thrives in the deep sandy granite soils found on the higher elevation gentle slopes.

The vineyard was converted from 20 years of conventional management to ACO-certified organic viticulture. It achieved Certified Organic status in 2022 (ACO 13305). Farming practices include cane pruning to open the canopy and reduce disease pressure, and gentle pruning principles that respect sap flow and old wood.

In December 2024, Small Wonder acquired a second 24-hectare vineyard in the Coal River Valley, which is currently in transition to organic certification. This newly acquired vineyard was planted between 2017 and 2019.

Varietals planted across the vineyards include predominantly Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, with smaller plantings of Riesling, Pinot Gris, and Sauvignon Blanc. The Coal River Valley vineyard also includes small amounts of Shiraz and Viognier.

## Cellar Door Experience
Small Wonder Wines offers tastings at their cellar door, which is nestled within native bushland on the Western Ridge of the Tamar Valley. Visitors can enjoy a relaxed tasting with locally sourced gourmet platters and views from the cellar door. Bookings are encouraged to avoid disappointment. The cellar door is open from 11 am to 5 pm daily, but will be closed on Tuesdays and Wednesdays from June 1, 2026, to August 31, 2026. A new, architecturally designed cellar door and restaurant are currently under construction and are set to open in early 2026. Guided winery tours are also offered.

## Food & Hospitality
Currently, the cellar door offers gourmet platters with locally sourced produce during tastings. A new, architecturally designed cellar door and restaurant are under construction and are expected to open in early 2026. This new facility will offer seasonal dishes and a space for memorable connections. The winery also aims to host tours and events.

## Awards & Recognition
Small Wonder Wines has received several awards and recognitions:
* **2026 Tasmanian Wine Show**: The 2025 Small Wonder Landscape Rosé was awarded the Labelhouse Trophy for Best Rosé. The 2021 Small Wonder Blanc de Noirs received a Gold Medal in the sparkling category. This marks Small Wonder's second consecutive year winning the Best Rosé trophy.
* **2025 Tasmanian Wine Show**: The 2024 Landscape Rosé won the Labelhouse Trophy for Best Rosé.
* **2024 Tasmanian Wine Show**: The 2021 Blanc de Blancs was awarded the House of Arras Trophy for Best Vintage Sparkling.
* **2023 Tasmanian Wine Show**: Received a Trophy for Best Sauvignon Blanc for their 2022 Sauvignon Blanc.
* **2025 Cathay Global Wine and Spirits Awards Asia**: The 2024 Small Wonder Landscape Chardonnay won a trophy for best Chardonnay in its class.
Small Wonder has been recognized with a trophy at the Tasmanian Wine Show for four consecutive years.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Small Wonder Wines is committed to reducing its environmental impact and respecting the land. The Tamar Valley vineyard was converted to ACO-certified organic viticulture and achieved certification in 2022 (ACO 13305). The newly acquired 24-hectare vineyard in the Coal River Valley is currently in transition to organic certification, with a commitment to achieve it within three years.

The purpose-built winery, completed in January 2024, is designed with energy efficiency and environmental credibility in mind. It is powered by green energy, supported by rainwater harvesting and storage, and features a best-practice wastewater system. The facility also includes solar panel-powered electricity generation and on-site electric vehicle charging stations. The surrounding land and vegetation management focused on preserving existing bushland and establishing additional natural habitat areas to encourage biodiversity. The winery's design incorporates passive strategies like natural ventilation and insulated precast concrete for thermal efficiency.

## Visiting Practical Info
**Address**: 530 Auburn Road, Kayena, Tasmania, 7270, Australia.
**Phone**: (03) 6394 7541.
**Website**: www.smallwonderwines.com.au.
**Social Media**: Instagram @small_wonder_wines.
**Distance from major towns**: Approximately a 35-minute drive from Launceston and a one-hour drive from the Spirit of Tasmania ferry terminal in Devonport. The Coal River Valley vineyard is 20 minutes from Hobart.
**Hours**: Open 11 am-5 pm daily. Closed Tuesday and Wednesday from June 1, 2026, to August 31, 2026.
Small Wonder participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme, allowing visitors to collect wines and have them shipped to their home address.

## Notable / Distinctive
Small Wonder Wines distinguishes itself by being a relatively new brand on a mature property, formerly known as Goaty Hill, that has undergone a significant transition to certified organic viticulture. The winery is family-owned and part of the Overstory group, which also owns Wayfinder in Margaret River, Western Australia, focusing on expressive, small-production wines from certified organic vineyards. Their commitment to organic certification extends to their newly acquired vineyard in the Coal River Valley. The purpose-built winery, completed in January 2024, is designed with a strong emphasis on sustainability, incorporating green energy, rainwater harvesting, and a state-of-the-art wastewater system. The upcoming architecturally designed cellar door and restaurant, set to open in early 2026, aims to create a thoughtful and contemporary destination. The winery's sustained success at the Tasmanian Wine Show, with trophies for four consecutive years, highlights the consistent quality across their portfolio.

